// Class 12 English - Vistas Chapter 2: The Tiger King (Kalki)
// Fully standardized to English Core Blueprint & AGENTS.md guidelines
// Theme Color: #FF6D00

export const c12EngV2HtmlOverview = "\n<style>\n  p, li, div:not(.table-container):not(.table-responsive):not(.pt-scroll-wrapper):not(.mcq-option) {\n    text-align: justify !important;\n  }\n  h1, h2, h3, h4, h5, h6 { text-align: left; }\n  .text-center { text-align: center !important; }\n  .text-left { text-align: left !important; }\n</style>\n\n<div style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, sans-serif; line-height: 1.8; color: #E2E8F0; max-width: 950px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 12px; font-size: 15.5px;\">\n\n  <!-- Chapter Header Badge -->\n  <div style=\"background: linear-gradient(135deg, rgba(255, 109, 0, 0.22), rgba(230, 81, 0, 0.1)); border: 1.5px solid #FF6D00; border-radius: 12px; padding: 20px; margin-bottom: 25px; box-shadow: 0 4px 22px rgba(255, 109, 0, 0.15);\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 10px; margin-bottom: 8px;\">\n      <span style=\"background: #FF6D00; color: #000; font-size: 12px; font-weight: 800; padding: 4px 10px; border-radius: 6px; letter-spacing: 0.5px;\">VISTAS &bull; CHAPTER 2</span>\n      <span style=\"color: #94A3B8; font-size: 13.5px; font-weight: 600;\">CLASS XII ENGLISH CORE (SUPPLEMENTARY READER)</span>\n    </div>\n    <h1 style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 26px; font-weight: 900; margin: 0 0 6px 0; letter-spacing: -0.5px;\">The Tiger King</h1>\n    <p style=\"color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 15px; margin: 0; font-style: italic;\">\n      By Kalki (1899&ndash;1954, Tamil Nadu) &mdash; Political Satire, Feudal Hubris &amp; The Supreme Irony of Fate\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Quick Glossary & Chapter Dossier Card -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.92); border: 1.5px solid rgba(255, 109, 0, 0.4); border-radius: 10px; padding: 18px 20px; margin-bottom: 25px; box-shadow: 0 4px 20px rgba(0,0,0,0.35);\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-size: 18px; margin: 0 0 12px 0; display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px;\">\n      <span>📖</span> Quick Glossary &amp; Chapter Dossier: The Tiger King\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(220px, 1fr)); gap: 12px; font-size: 14px; color: #CBD5E1;\">\n      <div><b style=\"color: #FF6D00;\">Author:</b> Kalki (Ramaswamy Aiyer Krishnamurthy, 1899&ndash;1954)</div>\n      <div><b style=\"color: #FF6D00;\">Genre:</b> Satirical Political Allegory / Ironical Folktale</div>\n      <div><b style=\"color: #FF6D00;\">Setting:</b> Pratibandapuram Princely State, Pre-Independence British India</div>\n      <div><b style=\"color: #FF6D00;\">Protagonist:</b> Sir Jilani Jung Jung Bahadur (The Tiger King)</div>\n      <div><b style=\"color: #FF6D00;\">Secondary Figures:</b> The Chief Astrologer, The Aged Dewan, Duraisani (British Officer's Wife)</div>\n      <div><b style=\"color: #FF6D00;\">Core Motif:</b> Human Hubris vs. Karmic Destiny; Despotic Cruelty to Wildlife</div>\n      <div><b style=\"color: #FF6D00;\">The Fatal Object:</b> A crude, two-anna wooden toy tiger's sliver</div>\n      <div><b style=\"color: #FF6D00;\">Literary Tone:</b> Pungent Satire, Grandiose Mock-Heroic Hyperbole, Tragicomic Irony</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Section 1: Executive Summary & Author Context -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-size: 20px; font-weight: 800; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF6D00; padding-bottom: 6px; margin: 28px 0 16px 0;\">\n    1. Executive Summary &amp; Satirical Framework\n  </h2>\n  <p>\n    <b style=\"color: #FF6D00;\">'The Tiger King'</b>, crafted by legendary Tamil writer, journalist, and freedom fighter <b style=\"color: #FF6D00;\">Kalki</b> (pen name of Ramaswamy Aiyer Krishnamurthy), stands as an immortal masterpiece of political satire, tragicomic folklore, and ecological allegory. Set in the colonial era of pre-independence India, the story skewers the autocratic arrogance, moral bankruptcy, and sycophantic servility that defined the princely rulers who governed under the protective umbrella of the British Raj.\n  </p>\n  <p>\n    The narrative chronicles the life and death of <b style=\"color: #FF6D00;\">Sir Jilani Jung Jung Bahadur</b>, the Maharaja of Pratibandapuram, universally remembered as the <b style=\"color: #FF6D00;\">Tiger King</b>. When royal astrologers predict at his birth that a tiger will cause his death, the king vows to hunt down and slaughter one hundred tigers to conquer his destiny. In his ruthless quest, he neglects his royal duties, taxes his citizens mercilessly, bribes British officials with royal diamonds, and wipes out the tiger population across two kingdoms. Yet, in a devastating twist of poetic justice, death arrives not from a fierce beast in the forest, but from a microscopic, infected wooden sliver detached from a cheap, two-anna toy tiger purchased for his son's birthday. Through biting irony, Kalki demonstrates that human arrogance cannot conquer mortality or circumvent the inevitable decree of nature.\n  </p>\n\n  <!-- Section 2: Chronological Narrative Architecture -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-size: 20px; font-weight: 800; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF6D00; padding-bottom: 6px; margin: 30px 0 16px 0;\">\n    2. Chronological &amp; Thematic Narrative Architecture\n  </h2>\n\n  <h3 style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-size: 17px; margin-top: 22px;\">(i) The Miraculous Prophecy at Ten Days Old</h3>\n  <p>\n    The narrative commences with the birth of the crown prince of Pratibandapuram. When court astrologers cast his horoscope, the Chief Astrologer solemnly declares that the child will grow into the warrior of warriors, hero of heroes, and champion of champions, but will ultimately have to die.\n  </p>\n  <p>\n    At this juncture, a breathtaking miracle occurs: the ten-day-old infant opens his lips and speaks in clear, articulate tones: <i>\"O wise astrologers!\"</i> The assembled courtiers and priests freeze in stunned disbelief. The infant astutely points out that everyone born into this world must eventually die; genuine astrological wisdom lies not in predicting death itself, but in disclosing the exact manner of that demise. Astonished, the Chief Astrologer places his finger upon his nose in wonderment and explains that because the prince was born under the hour of the <b style=\"color: #FF6D00;\">Bull</b>, and because the Bull and the <b style=\"color: #FF6D00;\">Tiger</b> are natural enemies, death will emanate from a tiger. Unfazed, the infant prince issues a chilling, haughty proclamation: <i>\"Let tigers beware!\"</i>\n  </p>\n\n  <h3 style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-size: 17px; margin-top: 22px;\">(ii) Pampered Colonial Upbringing</h3>\n  <p>\n    The young prince grew taller and stronger each day, raised in the Westernized opulence common to Indian princely heirs under British suzerainty. He drank the milk of an English cow, was tutored in English by an English gentleman, attended by an English nanny, and watched exclusively English cinema. At the age of twenty, the Court of Wards handed over the complete administrative reins of Pratibandapuram to the young Maharaja.\n  </p>\n\n  <h3 style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-size: 17px; margin-top: 22px;\">(iii) The Slaying of the First Tiger &amp; The Royal Vow</h3>\n  <p>\n    Remembering the childhood prophecy, the Maharaja initiated his tiger hunts. When he successfully shot his very first tiger, he summoned the Chief Astrologer in triumphant exultation. The aged seer remained unimpressed, observing: <i>\"Your Majesty may kill ninety-nine tigers in exactly the same manner. But you must be very careful with the hundredth tiger.\"</i> When the King demanded what would occur if he killed the hundredth tiger as well, the astrologer pledged to tear up all his astrological books, set fire to them, crop his hair, and become a life insurance agent.\n  </p>\n  <p>\n    From that day forward, the state banned tiger hunting for everyone except the Maharaja. A royal proclamation declared that anyone daring to fling so much as a stone at a tiger would have all their wealth and property confiscated. The King vowed he would attend to state administration only after exterminating his target of one hundred tigers.\n  </p>\n\n  <h3 style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-size: 17px; margin-top: 22px;\">(iv) The British Crisis &amp; The Diamond Rings</h3>\n  <p>\n    The King's throne was severely imperiled when a high-ranking British officer (<b style=\"color: #FF6D00;\">durai</b>) visited Pratibandapuram. The officer was exceedingly fond of hunting tigers, and even fonder of being photographed with the dead carcass gun in hand. The Maharaja firmly refused him permission, fearing that permitting one British officer would lead to dozens more demanding hunts.\n  </p>\n  <p>\n    To pacify the insulted colonial master and avert deposition, the King and his Dewan devised a desperate diplomatic bribe. They dispatched a telegram to a famous British jewelry firm in Calcutta, ordering fifty expensive <b style=\"color: #FF6D00;\">diamond rings</b> of diverse artistic designs. The entire consignment was sent to the British officer's wife (<b style=\"color: #FF6D00;\">duraisani</b>), expecting her to select one or two rings and return the rest. Instead, the greedy lady kept all fifty rings and sent a polite thank-you note! The bill amounted to <b style=\"color: #FF6D00;\">three lakh rupees</b>, but the Maharaja laughed heartily, content that his royal throne was saved.\n  </p>\n\n  <h3 style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-size: 17px; margin-top: 22px;\">(v) Matrimony for Tigers</h3>\n  <p>\n    Within ten years of relentless hunting, the Maharaja killed seventy tigers in Pratibandapuram, completely exhausting the forest population. Undeterred, he summoned the Dewan and announced his intention to marry. He instructed the Dewan to research royal genealogies and identify a princely state possessing the largest tiger population.\n  </p>\n  <p>\n    The Maharaja married the princess of a tiger-dense kingdom. Each time he visited his royal father-in-law, he slaughtered five or six tigers. In this calculated manner, ninety-nine tiger skins came to adorn the reception hall of the Pratibandapuram palace!\n  </p>\n\n  <h3 style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-size: 17px; margin-top: 22px;\">(vi) The Agony of the Missing Hundredth Tiger</h3>\n  <p>\n    Only one tiger remained to complete the tally of one hundred, but tigers had become completely extinct in his father-in-law's forests as well. The King sank into a dark, raging despair. Suddenly, news arrived that sheep were rapidly disappearing from a hillside village in his state, indicating the presence of a tiger. Overjoyed, the Maharaja announced a three-year tax exemption for the village and set out on the hunt.\n  </p>\n  <p>\n    However, the tiger proved elusive. Enraged, the King doubled the land tax, dismissed senior officers, and threatened the Dewan with immediate execution if the beast was not produced.\n  </p>\n\n  <h3 style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-size: 17px; margin-top: 22px;\">(vii) The Dewan's Comedic Deception</h3>\n  <p>\n    Fearing for his life, the aged Dewan retrieved an old, decrepit tiger that had been brought from the People's Park in Madras and kept concealed in his house. At midnight, the Dewan and his frail wife hauled the sluggish beast into their car, drove into the forest where the King was camping, and shoved the reluctant animal out into the brushwood.\n  </p>\n  <p>\n    The following morning, the Maharaja spotted the hundredth tiger, aimed with boundless joy, and fired. The tiger collapsed in a heap. Believing his vow was gloriously fulfilled, the King ordered the carcass brought to the capital in a grand triumphal procession and sped away in his royal car.\n  </p>\n  <p>\n    However, when the royal hunters stepped forward to examine the beast, the tiger opened its eyes and rolled them in bewildered amusement! The bullet had missed the tiger completely; the elderly beast had merely fainted from the thunderous crack of the gun! Terrified of losing their jobs if the King discovered the truth, one of the hunters took aim from one foot away and shot the tiger dead. The dead beast was paraded through the town and buried under a magnificent tomb.\n  </p>\n\n  <h3 style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-size: 17px; margin-top: 22px;\">(viii) The Fatal Wooden Sliver &amp; The Revenge of the Hundredth Tiger</h3>\n  <p>\n    A few days later was the third birthday of the Crown Prince. Having neglected his family during his tiger obsession, the King visited the market to purchase a special gift. He settled upon a cheap, hand-carved <b style=\"color: #FF6D00;\">wooden toy tiger</b>. It was a crude piece of carpentry, its rough surface covered with tiny raised slivers of wood. The shopkeeper, terrified of royal wrath, praised it as an exquisite specimen of craftsmanship and charged thirty rupees for a two-anna toy.\n  </p>\n  <p>\n    While the King was playing with his son, one of the sharp wooden splinters pierced the Maharaja's right hand. He pulled it out with his left hand and continued playing. The next day, virulent infection flared up; within four days, suppurating sores spread across his entire right arm.\n  </p>\n  <p>\n    Three renowned surgeons were summoned from Madras. After a complex surgical operation, the surgeons emerged from the theater and proclaimed with absurd medical irony: <i>\"The operation was successful. The Maharaja is dead.\"</i> In this manner, the hundredth tiger took its ultimate, poetic revenge upon the Tiger King!\n  </p>\n\n  <!-- Section 3: Psychological Character Profiles -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-size: 20px; font-weight: 800; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF6D00; padding-bottom: 6px; margin: 30px 0 16px 0;\">\n    3. Psychological Character Sketches &amp; Profiles\n  </h2>\n\n  <div style=\"display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(280px, 1fr)); gap: 16px; margin-bottom: 25px;\">\n    \n    <!-- The Tiger King -->\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 109, 0, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px;\">\n      <h3 style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-size: 17px; margin: 0 0 8px 0;\">1. The Tiger King (Sir Jilani Jung Jung Bahadur)</h3>\n      <p style=\"font-size: 14px; margin: 0; color: #CBD5E1;\">\n        The autocratic ruler of Pratibandapuram. He is characterized by overweening pride, tyrannical stubbornness, and reckless disregard for ecology and statecraft. Brave in physical combat but spiritually hollow, his entire identity revolves around defeating an astrological prediction. His tragic flaw is hubris&mdash;believing that royal authority and gunpowder can conquer nature and destiny.\n      </p>\n    </div>\n\n    <!-- The Dewan -->\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 109, 0, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px;\">\n      <h3 style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-size: 17px; margin: 0 0 8px 0;\">2. The Dewan (The Sycophantic Minister)</h3>\n      <p style=\"font-size: 14px; margin: 0; color: #CBD5E1;\">\n        The cowardly, subservient prime minister of the state. He exemplifies the servile bureaucrats who enabled royal despotism. Terrified of losing his position or life, he caters to every whimsical command of the King, from arranging a tiger-dense marriage to smuggling an old circus tiger into the jungle under cover of darkness.\n      </p>\n    </div>\n\n    <!-- Duraisani -->\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 109, 0, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px;\">\n      <h3 style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-size: 17px; margin: 0 0 8px 0;\">3. Duraisani (The Colonial Hypocrite)</h3>\n      <p style=\"font-size: 14px; margin: 0; color: #CBD5E1;\">\n        The wife of the British high-ranking officer. She satirizes British imperial greed and moral hypocrisy. Presented with fifty exquisite diamond rings intended as samples, she unashamedly pockets all fifty, sending a brief note of gratitude while costing the impoverished Indian state three lakh rupees.\n      </p>\n    </div>\n\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Section 4: Literary & Rhetorical Devices Master Table -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-size: 20px; font-weight: 800; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF6D00; padding-bottom: 6px; margin: 30px 0 16px 0;\">\n    4. Literary &amp; Rhetorical Devices Master Table\n  </h2>\n  <div style=\"overflow-x: auto; margin-bottom: 25px;\">\n    <table style=\"width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 14.5px; text-align: left; background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.9); border-radius: 8px; overflow: hidden;\">\n      <thead>\n        <tr style=\"background: rgba(255, 109, 0, 0.25); color: #FFFFFF; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF6D00;\">\n          <th style=\"padding: 12px 14px;\">Literary Device</th>\n          <th style=\"padding: 12px 14px;\">Textual Quote / Example</th>\n          <th style=\"padding: 12px 14px;\">Literary &amp; Thematic Effect</th>\n        </tr>\n      </thead>\n      <tbody>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\">\n          <td style=\"padding: 12px 14px; font-weight: bold; color: #FF6D00;\">Dramatic &amp; Cosmic Irony</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 12px 14px;\">The King slaughters ninety-nine ferocious tigers, but is killed by an inanimate, two-anna wooden toy tiger.</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 12px 14px; color: #CBD5E1;\">Highlights the absolute futility of human arrogance against the inexorable laws of karmic retribution.</td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\">\n          <td style=\"padding: 12px 14px; font-weight: bold; color: #FF6D00;\">Pungent Satire</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 12px 14px;\">\"The operation was successful. The Maharaja is dead.\"</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 12px 14px; color: #CBD5E1;\">Exposes the absurdity of medical vanity, sycophancy, and mock-heroic royal prestige.</td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\">\n          <td style=\"padding: 12px 14px; font-weight: bold; color: #FF6D00;\">Mock-Heroic Hyperbole</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 12px 14px;\">The staggering list of grandiose royal titles: <i>Jamedar-General, Khiledar-Major, Sata-Vyaghra Samhari</i>...</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 12px 14px; color: #CBD5E1;\">Ridicules the inflated vanity and pomposity of Indian princely rulers under colonial British rule.</td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\">\n          <td style=\"padding: 12px 14px; font-weight: bold; color: #FF6D00;\">Situational Humor</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 12px 14px;\">The old People's Park tiger fainting from the sound of gunshot; the Dewan and his wife dragging the tiger into the car.</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 12px 14px; color: #CBD5E1;\">Subverts the majestic myth of the royal hunt into a farcical circus of bureaucratic survival.</td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\">\n          <td style=\"padding: 12px 14px; font-weight: bold; color: #FF6D00;\">Ecological Allegory</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 12px 14px;\">The total extinction of tigers across two princely states purely to satisfy one man's vanity.</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 12px 14px; color: #CBD5E1;\">Condemns humanity's mindless exploitation and destruction of the natural world and wildlife.</td>\n        </tr>\n      </tbody>\n    </table>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Section 5: Board Exam Strategy & High-Scoring Tips Box -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.92); border-left: 5px solid #FF6D00; border-radius: 8px; padding: 18px 20px; margin-bottom: 25px; box-shadow: 0 4px 20px rgba(0,0,0,0.3);\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-size: 18px; margin: 0 0 10px 0; display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px;\">\n      <span>🎯</span> Board Examination Master Tips: Scoring 100% in The Tiger King\n    </h3>\n    <ul style=\"color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px; padding-left: 20px; margin: 0; line-height: 1.8;\">\n      <li><b style=\"color: #FF6D00;\">Identify Satirical Targets:</b> Clearly identify the twin targets of Kalki's satire: (1) despotic, self-absorbed Indian Maharajas, and (2) corrupt, hypocritical British colonial officers.</li>\n      <li><b style=\"color: #FF6D00;\">The 100th Tiger Technicality:</b> Remember that the King did NOT kill the hundredth tiger! It only fainted from the gunshot blast, and was later secretly killed by a hunter to protect his job.</li>\n      <li><b style=\"color: #FF6D00;\">Key Terms to Embed:</b> Always use literary concepts like <b style=\"color: #FF6D00;\">karmic retribution</b>, <b style=\"color: #FF6D00;\">hubris</b>, <b style=\"color: #FF6D00;\">mock-heroic hyperbole</b>, and <b style=\"color: #FF6D00;\">poetic justice</b>.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n\n</div>\n";

export const c12EngV2HtmlSolutions = "\n<style>\n  p, li, div:not(.table-container):not(.table-responsive):not(.pt-scroll-wrapper):not(.mcq-option) {\n    text-align: justify !important;\n  }\n  h1, h2, h3, h4, h5, h6 { text-align: left; }\n  .text-center { text-align: center !important; }\n  .text-left { text-align: left !important; }\n</style>\n\n<div style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, sans-serif; line-height: 1.8; color: #E2E8F0; max-width: 950px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 12px; font-size: 15.5px;\">\n\n  <!-- Section Header Badge -->\n  <div style=\"background: linear-gradient(135deg, rgba(255, 109, 0, 0.22), rgba(230, 81, 0, 0.1)); border-left: 5px solid #FF6D00; padding: 16px 20px; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 25px;\">\n    <h2 style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-weight: 900; margin: 0 0 4px 0; font-size: 21px;\">\n      BOARD EXAMINATION QUESTIONS &amp; MODEL ANSWERS\n    </h2>\n    <span style=\"color: #94A3B8; font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600;\">Standardized to CBSE &amp; JKBOSE Class XII English Core Blueprint &bull; Theme Color Standardized</span>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION A: EXTRACT-BASED QUESTIONS -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-size: 20px; font-weight: 800; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF6D00; padding-bottom: 6px; margin: 26px 0 16px 0;\">\n    SECTION A: Extract-Based Objective Questions (1 Mark Each)\n  </h2>\n\n  <!-- Extract 1 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.5px solid rgba(255, 109, 0, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px; border-left: 4px solid #FF6D00;\">\n    <p style=\"color: #94A3B8; font-style: italic; margin-top: 0; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF6D00;\">Read the extract given below and answer the questions that follow:</b><br/>\n      \"The infant said, 'All those who are born will one day have to die. We don't need your predictions to know that. There would be some sense in it if you could tell us the manner of that death.' The chief astrologer placed his finger on his nose in wonder. A ten-day-old infant opening its lips in speech!\"\n    </p>\n    \n    <p>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-size: 15px;\">Q1: Who was speaking with such astonishing philosophical clarity?</b><br/>\n      <span style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> The ten-day-old infant royal prince of Pratibandapuram, later famously known as the <b style=\"color: #FF6D00;\">Tiger King</b>.\n    </p>\n\n    <p>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-size: 15px;\">Q2: What physical gesture demonstrated the Chief Astrologer's profound disbelief?</b><br/>\n      <span style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> The Chief Astrologer placed his finger upon his nose in sheer astonishment and awe.\n    </p>\n\n    <p>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-size: 15px;\">Q3: What logical distinction did the infant draw regarding genuine astrological wisdom?</b><br/>\n      <span style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> He stated that predicting universal mortality holds no wisdom; genuine foresight lies in disclosing the <b style=\"color: #FF6D00;\">precise manner of death</b>.\n    </p>\n\n    <p>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-size: 15px;\">Q4: What warning did the infant prince issue immediately after hearing the prediction?</b><br/>\n      <span style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> He issued the haughty, memorable royal challenge: <i>\"Let tigers beware!\"</i>\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Extract 2 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.5px solid rgba(255, 109, 0, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px; border-left: 4px solid #FF6D00;\">\n    <p style=\"color: #94A3B8; font-style: italic; margin-top: 0; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF6D00;\">Read the extract given below and answer the questions that follow:</b><br/>\n      \"The Maharaja vowed he would attend to all other matters only after killing the hundred tigers. Initially the king seemed well set to realise his ambition. Not that he faced no dangers. There were times when the bullet missed its mark, the tiger leapt upon him and he fought the beast with his bare hands. Each time it was the Maharaja who won.\"\n    </p>\n    \n    <p>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-size: 15px;\">Q5: What resolution did the Maharaja adopt regarding state administration?</b><br/>\n      <span style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> He resolved to suspend all administrative state affairs until he had slaughtered <b style=\"color: #FF6D00;\">one hundred tigers</b>.\n    </p>\n\n    <p>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-size: 15px;\">Q6: What physical perils did the Maharaja confront during his hunting expeditions?</b><br/>\n      <span style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> Bullets occasionally missed their target, compelling him to fight ferocious tigers barehanded in deadly combat.\n    </p>\n\n    <p>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-size: 15px;\">Q7: What does this extract reveal about the Maharaja's physical bravery?</b><br/>\n      <span style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> Though an arrogant despot, he possessed undeniable physical courage and formidable combat prowess.\n    </p>\n\n    <p>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-size: 15px;\">Q8: How many tigers did the Maharaja successfully hunt in his own kingdom before they became extinct?</b><br/>\n      <span style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> He successfully killed <b style=\"color: #FF6D00;\">seventy tigers</b> within ten years in Pratibandapuram.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Extract 3 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.5px solid rgba(255, 109, 0, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px; border-left: 4px solid #FF6D00;\">\n    <p style=\"color: #94A3B8; font-style: italic; margin-top: 0; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF6D00;\">Read the extract given below and answer the questions that follow:</b><br/>\n      \"The Maharaja and the dewan held deliberations over this issue. As a result, a telegram was dispatched to a famous British company of jewellers in Calcutta: 'Send samples of expensive diamond rings of different designs.' Some fifty rings arrived.\"\n    </p>\n    \n    <p>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-size: 15px;\">Q9: What political crisis prompted the Maharaja to order these diamond rings?</b><br/>\n      <span style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> The King had refused tiger hunting permission to a British officer, endangering his royal throne and state sovereignty.\n    </p>\n\n    <p>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-size: 15px;\">Q10: Who were these fifty diamond rings dispatched to?</b><br/>\n      <span style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> They were dispatched to the British officer's wife, known respectfully as the <b style=\"color: #FF6D00;\">duraisani</b>.\n    </p>\n\n    <p>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-size: 15px;\">Q11: How did the lady respond to the consignment, and what was the cost?</b><br/>\n      <span style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> She greedily kept all fifty rings, costing the royal exchequer <b style=\"color: #FF6D00;\">three lakh rupees</b>.\n    </p>\n\n    <p>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-size: 15px;\">Q12: What aspect of British colonial governance does this incident satirize?</b><br/>\n      <span style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> It exposes the shameless bribery, moral corruption, and hypocrisy of British colonial dignitaries.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Extract 4 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.5px solid rgba(255, 109, 0, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px; border-left: 4px solid #FF6D00;\">\n    <p style=\"color: #94A3B8; font-style: italic; margin-top: 0; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF6D00;\">Read the extract given below and answer the questions that follow:</b><br/>\n      \"The surgeons who were summoned from Madras performed the operation. When they finished, they announced: 'The operation was successful. The Maharaja is dead.' In this manner the hundredth tiger took its final revenge upon the Tiger King.\"\n    </p>\n    \n    <p>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-size: 15px;\">Q13: From where were the surgical specialists summoned to treat the King?</b><br/>\n      <span style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> Three eminent surgeons were summoned from <b style=\"color: #FF6D00;\">Madras</b>.\n    </p>\n\n    <p>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-size: 15px;\">Q14: What was the physical source of the fatal infection that afflicted the King?</b><br/>\n      <span style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> A tiny, sharp wooden sliver from a crude, two-anna toy tiger purchased for his son's birthday.\n    </p>\n\n    <p>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-size: 15px;\">Q15: Explain the pungent satirical humor in the surgeons' declaration.</b><br/>\n      <span style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> Declaring an operation successful while the patient died satirizes professional vanity and dark medical irony.\n    </p>\n\n    <p>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-size: 15px;\">Q16: How did the 'hundredth tiger' take its poetic revenge?</b><br/>\n      <span style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> The wooden toy tiger, functioning as the actual hundredth tiger, inflicted the mortal infection that ended his life.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION B: SHORT ANSWER QUESTIONS -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-size: 20px; font-weight: 800; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF6D00; padding-bottom: 6px; margin: 30px 0 16px 0;\">\n    SECTION B: Short Answer Questions (3 Marks Each | 40&ndash;50 Words)\n  </h2>\n\n  <!-- Q17 -->\n  <div style=\"margin-bottom: 20px; background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.65); padding: 14px 16px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 4px solid #FF6D00;\">\n    <h4 style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-size: 16px; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-weight: 700;\">Q17: What prophecy was made by the Chief Astrologer at the birth of the prince?</h4>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #CBD5E1; line-height: 1.75;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> The Chief Astrologer foretold that the prince would become a supreme warrior and hero, but would die because he was born in the hour of the <b style=\"color: #FF6D00;\">Bull</b>. Since the Bull and the <b style=\"color: #FF6D00;\">Tiger</b> are natural enemies, his ultimate death would come from a tiger.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q18 -->\n  <div style=\"margin-bottom: 20px; background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.65); padding: 14px 16px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 4px solid #FF6D00;\">\n    <h4 style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-size: 16px; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-weight: 700;\">Q18: How was the royal infant brought up until he attained the age of twenty?</h4>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #CBD5E1; line-height: 1.75;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> The prince was brought up in pampered Westernized opulence. He drank the milk of an English cow, was tutored in English by an Englishman, attended by an English nanny, and watched exclusively English films, until the Court of Wards handed him the crown at twenty.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q19 -->\n  <div style=\"margin-bottom: 20px; background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.65); padding: 14px 16px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 4px solid #FF6D00;\">\n    <h4 style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-size: 16px; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-weight: 700;\">Q19: What pledge did the Chief Astrologer make when challenged by the Maharaja?</h4>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #CBD5E1; line-height: 1.75;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> When the King asked what would occur if he killed the hundredth tiger as well, the astrologer pledged to tear up all his astrological books, burn them to ashes, crop his hair short, and become a life insurance agent.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q20 -->\n  <div style=\"margin-bottom: 20px; background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.65); padding: 14px 16px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 4px solid #FF6D00;\">\n    <h4 style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-size: 16px; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-weight: 700;\">Q20: Why did the Maharaja ban tiger hunting by anyone else in Pratibandapuram?</h4>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #CBD5E1; line-height: 1.75;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> The Maharaja reserved all tigers exclusively for himself to fulfill his target of slaying one hundred beasts. He issued a royal decree that anyone throwing even a stone at a tiger would forfeit all their wealth and property to the state.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q21 -->\n  <div style=\"margin-bottom: 20px; background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.65); padding: 14px 16px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 4px solid #FF6D00;\">\n    <h4 style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-size: 16px; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-weight: 700;\">Q21: How did the Maharaja avert the crisis of losing his throne to the British officer?</h4>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #CBD5E1; line-height: 1.75;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> The King ordered fifty expensive diamond rings from Calcutta and sent them to the British officer's wife (<b style=\"color: #FF6D00;\">duraisani</b>). The greedy lady kept all fifty rings, costing the state three lakh rupees, but successfully pacified the officer and preserved the throne.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q22 -->\n  <div style=\"margin-bottom: 20px; background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.65); padding: 14px 16px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 4px solid #FF6D00;\">\n    <h4 style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-size: 16px; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-weight: 700;\">Q22: Why did the Maharaja decide to marry, and how did his marriage serve his ambition?</h4>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #CBD5E1; line-height: 1.75;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> Having exterminated all seventy tigers in Pratibandapuram, the King instructed the Dewan to find a royal bride whose kingdom possessed the highest tiger population. Whenever he visited his father-in-law, he shot five or six tigers, bringing his tally to ninety-nine.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q23 -->\n  <div style=\"margin-bottom: 20px; background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.65); padding: 14px 16px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 4px solid #FF6D00;\">\n    <h4 style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-size: 16px; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-weight: 700;\">Q23: Why did the Maharaja double the land tax on the hillside village?</h4>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #CBD5E1; line-height: 1.75;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> When sheep began disappearing from a hillside village, the King initially granted a three-year tax exemption expecting to shoot his hundredth tiger. When the tiger could not be located, his frustration turned to fury, and he vindictively doubled the village's land tax.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q24 -->\n  <div style=\"margin-bottom: 20px; background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.65); padding: 14px 16px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 4px solid #FF6D00;\">\n    <h4 style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-size: 16px; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-weight: 700;\">Q24: How was the hundredth tiger procured for the Maharaja's final hunt?</h4>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #CBD5E1; line-height: 1.75;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> Fearing execution by the enraged King, the Dewan brought an old, decrepit tiger from the People's Park in Madras. At midnight, he and his wife loaded the sluggish beast into their car, drove to the jungle, and abandoned it near the King's camp.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q25 -->\n  <div style=\"margin-bottom: 20px; background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.65); padding: 14px 16px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 4px solid #FF6D00;\">\n    <h4 style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-size: 16px; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-weight: 700;\">Q25: What truth about the hundredth tiger was concealed from the Maharaja?</h4>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #CBD5E1; line-height: 1.75;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> The Maharaja's bullet missed the hundredth tiger entirely; the feeble animal had merely fainted from the sound of the gunshot. The royal hunters kept this secret out of fear of dismissal, and one hunter shot the beast dead from point-blank range.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q26 -->\n  <div style=\"margin-bottom: 20px; background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.65); padding: 14px 16px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 4px solid #FF6D00;\">\n    <h4 style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-size: 16px; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-weight: 700;\">Q26: Why did the shopkeeper overcharge the Maharaja for the wooden toy tiger?</h4>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #CBD5E1; line-height: 1.75;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> The wooden toy tiger was worth only two annas. However, the shopkeeper knew that offering a cheap gift to the despotic Maharaja would invite severe punishment under emergency laws, so he praised it as a rare masterwork and charged thirty rupees.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q27 -->\n  <div style=\"margin-bottom: 20px; background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.65); padding: 14px 16px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 4px solid #FF6D00;\">\n    <h4 style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-size: 16px; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-weight: 700;\">Q27: How did a tiny sliver of wood lead to the Maharaja's death?</h4>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #CBD5E1; line-height: 1.75;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> The crude wooden tiger had tiny wood splinters standing up like quills. One splinter pierced the King's right palm. A virulent infection spread rapidly, turning into a suppurating sore that infected his entire arm and proved fatal despite surgery.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q28 -->\n  <div style=\"margin-bottom: 20px; background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.65); padding: 14px 16px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 4px solid #FF6D00;\">\n    <h4 style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-size: 16px; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-weight: 700;\">Q28: Explain the significance of the title 'The Tiger King'.</h4>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #CBD5E1; line-height: 1.75;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> The title is deeply ironical. It ostensibly celebrates the Maharaja's obsessive slaughter of ninety-nine royal beasts, but ultimately highlights his pathetic mortality, as this mighty 'Tiger King' is defeated by an inanimate sliver of wood from a toy tiger.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION C: LONG ANSWER QUESTIONS -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-size: 20px; font-weight: 800; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF6D00; padding-bottom: 6px; margin: 30px 0 16px 0;\">\n    SECTION C: Long Answer / Thematic Questions (6 Marks Each | 120&ndash;150 Words)\n  </h2>\n\n  <!-- Q29 -->\n  <div style=\"margin-bottom: 22px; background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.5px solid rgba(255, 109, 0, 0.35); border-radius: 10px; padding: 18px; box-shadow: 0 4px 20px rgba(0,0,0,0.3);\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-size: 17px; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-weight: 800;\">\n      Q29: How does Kalki use irony and satire to expose the vanity of power and the inevitability of destiny in 'The Tiger King'?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #CBD5E1; line-height: 1.8; margin: 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF6D00;\">Model Answer:</b><br/>\n      <b>1. Introduction &amp; Satirical Framework:</b> In 'The Tiger King', Kalki masterfully employs biting political satire and cosmic irony to mock the autocratic vanity of Indian princely rulers. The Maharaja of Pratibandapuram, armed with absolute power, absurdly believes he can conquer mortality and outwit destiny by slaughtering one hundred tigers.<br/><br/>\n      <b>2. The Mechanics of Satire &amp; Irony:</b> Kalki skewers royal hubris through multiple satirical episodes. The King abandons governance, doubles peasant taxes out of personal fury, and spends three lakh rupees bribing a British officer's wife with diamond rings to protect his hunting privileges. The dramatic irony reaches its peak during the hunt of the hundredth tiger: the King rejoices over fulfilling his vow, unaware that his bullet missed and the old beast merely fainted from the gun's blast.<br/><br/>\n      <b>3. Cosmic Retribution &amp; Conclusion:</b> The ultimate stroke of situational irony occurs when death claims the monarch not through a fearsome predator in the forest, but via a two-anna crude wooden toy tiger. A tiny wooden sliver causes a fatal suppurating infection, fulfilling the astrological prophecy with devastating perfection. Kalki thus demonstrates that mortal power cannot triumph over nature or destiny.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q30 -->\n  <div style=\"margin-bottom: 22px; background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.5px solid rgba(255, 109, 0, 0.35); border-radius: 10px; padding: 18px; box-shadow: 0 4px 20px rgba(0,0,0,0.3);\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-size: 17px; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-weight: 800;\">\n      Q30: Comment on the sycophancy and moral cowardice of the courtiers and officials in the story.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #CBD5E1; line-height: 1.8; margin: 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF6D00;\">Model Answer:</b><br/>\n      <b>1. Introduction:</b> Kalki's 'The Tiger King' is as much an indictment of craven bureaucratic sycophancy as it is of royal megalomania. The courtiers, ministers, and hunters surrounding the Maharaja operate out of sheer terror and self-preservation, abandoning all integrity and truth.<br/><br/>\n      <b>2. The Dewan &amp; Courtiers' Cowardice:</b> The Dewan represents the archetype of the servile official. Instead of advising the King against neglecting his kingdom or slaughtering wildlife, he enables his obsession. He facilitates a marriage based purely on tiger statistics and secretly smuggles an ancient circus tiger from Madras to avoid royal wrath. When the hundredth tiger faints rather than dies, the hunters deliberately conceal the truth from the King, shooting the helpless beast themselves to preserve their livelihood.<br/><br/>\n      <b>3. Critical Conclusion:</b> This pervasive sycophancy insulated the King from reality, feeding his delusional omnipotence. Kalki highlights how totalitarian rule flourishes when civil servants capitulate to fear and abandon moral responsibility.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q31 -->\n  <div style=\"margin-bottom: 22px; background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.5px solid rgba(255, 109, 0, 0.35); border-radius: 10px; padding: 18px; box-shadow: 0 4px 20px rgba(0,0,0,0.3);\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-size: 17px; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-weight: 800;\">\n      Q31: What portrait of British colonial rule and Anglo-Indian princely relationships emerges in 'The Tiger King'?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #CBD5E1; line-height: 1.8; margin: 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF6D00;\">Model Answer:</b><br/>\n      <b>1. Colonial Power Dynamics:</b> Kalki paints a sharp, satirical portrait of the complex relationship between British colonial masters and native Indian princes under the Raj. While Maharajas exercised tyrannical authority over their own subjects, they remained subservient vassals to the British Crown, living in constant terror of deposition.<br/><br/>\n      <b>2. Superficial Vanity &amp; Imperial Corruption:</b> The British officer (durai) is satirized for his superficial vanity&mdash;his primary passion is not hunting per se, but posing triumphantly for photographs with a dead tiger and gun in hand. When the King denies him this privilege, the entire state is plunged into political peril. The crisis is resolved not through diplomacy, but through blatant imperial bribery: fifty diamond rings sent to the officer's wife, who unashamedly pockets the entire fortune.<br/><br/>\n      <b>3. Conclusion:</b> Kalki exposes the moral hollowness of British imperial dignity. Beneath the facade of Victorian discipline lay unchecked greed, while Indian princes squandered their nation's wealth to flatter their colonial overlords.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q32 -->\n  <div style=\"margin-bottom: 22px; background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.5px solid rgba(255, 109, 0, 0.35); border-radius: 10px; padding: 18px; box-shadow: 0 4px 20px rgba(0,0,0,0.3);\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-size: 17px; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-weight: 800;\">\n      Q32: Discuss 'The Tiger King' as an ecological allegory condemning humanity's callous destruction of wildlife.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #CBD5E1; line-height: 1.8; margin: 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF6D00;\">Model Answer:</b><br/>\n      <b>1. Introduction:</b> Beneath its satirical and humorous exterior, Kalki's 'The Tiger King' functions as a profound ecological allegory warning against anthropocentric arrogance and the ruthless annihilation of the natural world.<br/><br/>\n      <b>2. Senseless Ecological Annihilation:</b> The Maharaja views tigers not as magnificent apex predators essential to the ecological web, but merely as inanimate milestones to satisfy his personal ego and survive an astrological threat. In ten years, he drives Pratibandapuram's tiger population to extinction, and subsequently wipes out the tigers in his father-in-law's kingdom. Forest ecosystems are stripped bare solely to fulfill one tyrant's paranoid obsession.<br/><br/>\n      <b>3. Nature's Retribution:</b> Nature, however, exacts poetically balanced justice. The mighty hunter who extinguished ninety-nine magnificent living tigers is struck down by an inanimate wooden representation of the very species he persecuted. Kalki delivers a powerful moral message: whenever humanity wages war against nature, nature ultimately claims the final victory.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q33 -->\n  <div style=\"margin-bottom: 22px; background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.5px solid rgba(255, 109, 0, 0.35); border-radius: 10px; padding: 18px; box-shadow: 0 4px 20px rgba(0,0,0,0.3);\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-size: 17px; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-weight: 800;\">\n      Q33: Analyze the comedic and farcical elements surrounding the procurement and shooting of the hundredth tiger.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #CBD5E1; line-height: 1.8; margin: 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF6D00;\">Model Answer:</b><br/>\n      <b>1. Comic Inversion of the Royal Hunt:</b> Kalki transforms the climax of the King's life mission into pure slapstick farce, subverting the heroic myth of the tiger hunt into a grotesque comedy of errors.<br/><br/>\n      <b>2. The Midnight Circus Tiger:</b> The procurement of the hundredth tiger is devoid of any royal dignity. Terrified of losing his head, the aged Dewan smuggles a senile, toothless tiger from Madras People's Park into his private residence. At midnight, the Dewan and his frail wife comical struggle to drag the reluctant beast into their automobile and shove it into the forest brushwood.<br/><br/>\n      <b>3. The Fainting Beast &amp; Cover-up:</b> The next morning, when the King shoots, the feeble beast simply faints from the loud gunshot. The hunter who was supposed to be a legendary warrior walks away in triumphant delusion, while his panicked staff secretly executes the unconscious animal to protect their employment. This masterly comedy underlines the utter absurdity of authoritarian rule.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q34 -->\n  <div style=\"margin-bottom: 22px; background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.5px solid rgba(255, 109, 0, 0.35); border-radius: 10px; padding: 18px; box-shadow: 0 4px 20px rgba(0,0,0,0.3);\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-size: 17px; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-weight: 800;\">\n      Q34: How does the ending of 'The Tiger King' deliver poetic justice?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #CBD5E1; line-height: 1.8; margin: 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF6D00;\">Model Answer:</b><br/>\n      <b>1. Concept of Poetic Justice:</b> Poetic justice demands that virtue is rewarded and vice or hubris is fittingly punished by the direct consequences of one's own transgressions. The conclusion of 'The Tiger King' delivers one of the most celebrated examples of poetic justice in Indian literature.<br/><br/>\n      <b>2. The Instrument of Karma:</b> The Maharaja spent his entire life shooting majestic tigers with modern rifles, believing he had conquered fate after the supposed demise of the hundredth tiger. Yet karma utilizes the humblest and most ironic instrument: a crude wooden toy tiger purchased for his son's birthday. A jagged sliver left behind by an underpaid, terrified carpenter pierces the King's flesh, inducing a mortal infection.<br/><br/>\n      <b>3. The Supreme Satirical Verdict:</b> The announcement of the surgeons&mdash;<i>\"The operation was successful. The Maharaja is dead\"</i>&mdash;cements the cosmic joke. The beast he slaughtered across two kingdoms triumphs through an inanimate effigy, proving that human vanity cannot evade karmic reckoning.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION D: NCERT TEXTBOOK EXERCISES -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-size: 20px; font-weight: 800; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF6D00; padding-bottom: 6px; margin: 30px 0 16px 0;\">\n    SECTION D: NCERT Textbook Exercises (Reading With Insight)\n  </h2>\n\n  <!-- NCERT Q1 -->\n  <div style=\"margin-bottom: 18px; background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.55); border-left: 4px solid #FF6D00; padding: 14px 16px; border-radius: 6px;\">\n    <h4 style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-size: 15.5px; margin: 0 0 6px 0; font-weight: 700;\">Q35 (NCERT 1): Who is the Tiger King? Why does he get that name?</h4>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #CBD5E1; line-height: 1.75;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> The Tiger King is Sir Jilani Jung Jung Bahadur, the Maharaja of Pratibandapuram. He acquired this moniker because court astrologers predicted at his birth that his death would be caused by a tiger. In defiance, the prince challenged all tigers, vowed to slay one hundred beasts before attending to statecraft, and spent his life ruthlessly hunting tigers across two kingdoms.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- NCERT Q2 -->\n  <div style=\"margin-bottom: 18px; background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.55); border-left: 4px solid #FF6D00; padding: 14px 16px; border-radius: 6px;\">\n    <h4 style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-size: 15.5px; margin: 0 0 6px 0; font-weight: 700;\">Q36 (NCERT 2): What did the royal infant grow up to be?</h4>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #FF6D00; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> The royal infant grew up to become a tall, powerfully built, autocratic Maharaja. Pampered in Westernized royal decadence with English cows' milk, English tutors, and English films, he assumed power at twenty and developed into an obsessive, ruthless huntsman and tyrant whose entire administrative reign was consumed by his quest to exterminate tigers.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- NCERT Q3 -->\n  <div style=\"margin-bottom: 18px; background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.55); border-left: 4px solid #FF6D00; padding: 14px 16px; border-radius: 6px;\">\n    <h4 style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-size: 15.5px; margin: 0 0 6px 0; font-weight: 700;\">Q37 (NCERT 3): What will the Maharaja do to find the required number of tigers to kill?</h4>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #CBD5E1; line-height: 1.75;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> After exterminating all seventy tigers in his own forests, the Maharaja commanded his Dewan to locate a royal princess from a state possessing the densest tiger population. He married the princess and hunted five or six tigers on every visit to his father-in-law, successfully bagging twenty-nine additional tigers to reach ninety-nine.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- NCERT Q4 -->\n  <div style=\"margin-bottom: 18px; background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.55); border-left: 4px solid #FF6D00; padding: 14px 16px; border-radius: 6px;\">\n    <h4 style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-size: 15.5px; margin: 0 0 6px 0; font-weight: 700;\">Q38 (NCERT 4): How will the Maharaja prepare himself for the hundredth tiger which was supposed to decide his final fate?</h4>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #CBD5E1; line-height: 1.75;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #FF6D00; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> The Maharaja became totally consumed by feverish anxiety over the hundredth tiger. Hearing reports of disappearing sheep from a hillside village, he granted a three-year tax exemption and set up camp in the forest. When the tiger proved elusive, he doubled taxes in fury and threatened his Dewan with execution, ultimately shooting an old circus tiger planted in the jungle by the desperate minister.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n</div>\n";

